{%- comment -%}
  sections/lada-lookbook.liquid

  The ninth section, and the only one on the page that carries no
  commerce furniture at all: no captions, no prices, no add-to-cart, no
  badges, no hotspots. Six to nine frames, edge to edge, and that is
  the whole content model. Naushemian's /farhaad-2026 runs 41 frames at
  native resolution with the narrative parked at the very end and six
  frames quietly wrapped in an anchor, and it converts precisely
  because it never once looks like a shop.

  THE EMPTY STATE IS THE STATE THIS SHIPS IN. There is exactly one Lada
  photograph in the theme today and it is already spent on the hero, so
  the honest default is nothing at all. With no block carrying an image
  this section renders zero markup on the live page: not an empty
  scroller, not a row of grey placeholder boxes, not a 2px gutter
  hanging in the dark. The preset lays out the eight-frame rhythm (six
  2:3 portraits, two 3:2 landscapes) as empty blocks so the client only
  has to drop pictures into a shape that is already correct, and each
  frame appears the moment its own picture arrives.

  BUT ZERO MARKUP IS ALSO ZERO EDITOR. The first pass hid the section
  unconditionally, and that broke the one thing this section exists to
  support: the client filling it himself later. In the theme editor a
  section that renders nothing cannot be seen, cannot be positioned,
  and, because block.shopify_attributes only ships on markup that
  actually renders, its empty frame blocks cannot be clicked, named or
  reordered. The client would have added eight frames and been staring
  at the gap between lada-craft and lada-measure. So the guard is now
  split: the live storefront still renders nothing until a photograph
  arrives, and request.design_mode renders a ghost per empty block plus
  a one-line notice saying the section is hidden until then. Nothing in
  the design_mode branch can reach a customer, which is why those two
  strings are hard coded rather than settings.

  SNAP LIVES ON THE DOCUMENT, NOT ON THIS DIV. scroll-snap-type only
  does anything on an element that actually scrolls, so putting it on
  .lada__frames would have been a no-op that read correct in review and
  did nothing in the browser. The rule is written as
  html:has(.lada__lookbook) in the stylesheet instead, which is the same
  behaviour the spec asks for without opening a nested scroller inside
  the page. Proximity, never mandatory, and never a wheel handler: Sana
  Safinaz's mousewheel capture with releaseOnEdges:false is what breaks
  find-in-page, deep linking, keyboard and trackpad users, and its own
  teardown names scroll-snap as the replacement.

  ANCHORS ARE BARE, BUT THEY ARE NOT ANONYMOUS. A linked frame gets
  cursor:pointer and a focus ring and nothing else: no arrow, no
  overlay, no hover label. The accessible name is the alt text, which
  describes the photograph the customer is clicking. The first pass
  fell back to a link_label that shipped defaulted to "See this piece",
  which meant the spec's three linked frames all announced the same
  four words while pointing at three different products: a screen
  reader user hears one link repeated and cannot tell the destinations
  apart. The default is gone, the field prompts for the piece's own
  name, and if it is still blank the frame number keeps the names
  distinct rather than colliding.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign frame_count = 0
  for block in section.blocks
    if block.settings.image != blank or block.settings.asset_file != blank
      assign frame_count = frame_count | plus: 1
    endif
  endfor

  # Live: a lookbook with no photographs is not a lookbook, so it is not
  # drawn. Editor: always drawn, so the client can find and fill it.
  assign render_section = false
  if frame_count > 0 or request.design_mode
    assign render_section = true
  endif
-%}

{%- if render_section -%}
  <section class="lada lada__lookbook{% if section.settings.cap_height %} lada__lookbook--capped{% endif %}"
           id="lada-lookbook"
           aria-label="{{ section.settings.region_label | default: 'Lada lookbook' | escape }}">

    {%- if request.design_mode and frame_count == 0 -%}
      <p class="lada__lookbook-editor">Editor only. This section stays hidden on the live page until at least one frame has a photograph. Drop a picture into any frame below and it appears.</p>
    {%- endif -%}

    <div class="lada__frames">
      {%- assign frame_n = 0 -%}
      {%- for block in section.blocks -%}
        {%- liquid
          assign img = block.settings.image
          assign asset = block.settings.asset_file

          assign filled = false
          if img != blank or asset != blank
            assign filled = true
          endif

          assign shape = 'lada__frame--tall'
          assign shape_word = 'portrait'
          assign fallback_w = 1200
          assign fallback_h = 1800
          if block.settings.ratio == 'landscape'
            assign shape = 'lada__frame--wide'
            assign shape_word = 'landscape'
            assign fallback_w = 1800
            assign fallback_h = 1200
          endif
        -%}

        {%- comment -%}
          A block the client has added but not yet filled is not a frame.
          Skipping it rather than rendering an empty box is what keeps a
          half-filled lookbook looking edited. In the editor it still
          needs a body to hang shopify_attributes on, otherwise the
          client cannot select the block he is trying to fill, so the
          ghost stands in there and only there.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- unless filled -%}
          {%- if request.design_mode -%}
            <div class="lada__frame lada__frame--ghost {{ shape }}" {{ block.shopify_attributes }}>
              <span class="lada__ghost-l">Frame {{ forloop.index }}, {{ shape_word }}, no photograph yet</span>
            </div>
          {%- endif -%}
          {%- continue -%}
        {%- endunless -%}

        {%- liquid
          assign frame_n = frame_n | plus: 1

          assign alt = block.settings.alt
          if alt == blank and img != blank
            assign alt = img.alt
          endif

          # Only a linked frame needs a name of its own, and only when the
          # alt is blank. Falling back to the frame number is poor copy but
          # it is at least unique, which a shared default never is.
          assign link_name = ''
          if block.settings.link != blank and alt == blank
            assign link_name = block.settings.link_label
            if link_name == blank
              assign link_name = 'Lada piece, frame ' | append: frame_n
            endif
          endif
        -%}

        {%- capture frame_media -%}
          {%- if img != blank -%}
            {%- comment -%}
              Never ask Shopify for more pixels than the file holds. The
              candidates are emitted only while they are strictly smaller
              than the source, and the source's own width closes the list,
              so a 1400px photograph offers 800w and 1400w rather than an
              upscaled 1800w that can only come back soft. width/height are
              the real intrinsic numbers, so the box is reserved before the
              bytes land and a nine-frame run does not shift under the
              reader.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- assign nat = img.width -%}
            {%- if nat > 2400 %}{% assign nat = 2400 %}{% endif -%}
            {%- capture frame_srcset -%}
              {%- if nat > 800 %}{{ img | image_url: width: 800 }} 800w, {% endif -%}
              {%- if nat > 1200 %}{{ img | image_url: width: 1200 }} 1200w, {% endif -%}
              {%- if nat > 1800 %}{{ img | image_url: width: 1800 }} 1800w, {% endif -%}
              {{ img | image_url: width: nat }} {{ nat }}w
            {%- endcapture -%}
            <img src="{{ img | image_url: width: nat }}"
                 srcset="{{ frame_srcset | strip_newlines | strip }}"
                 sizes="100vw"
                 width="{{ img.width }}" height="{{ img.height }}"
                 loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                 alt="{{ alt | escape }}">
          {%- else -%}
            {%- comment -%}
              A theme asset cannot report its own dimensions, so the box is
              reserved from the shape the client picked. object-fit:cover
              absorbs the difference if the file is cut slightly off.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <img src="{{ asset | asset_url }}"
                 width="{{ fallback_w }}" height="{{ fallback_h }}"
                 loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                 alt="{{ alt | escape }}">
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- endcapture -%}

        <figure class="lada__frame {{ shape }}" {{ block.shopify_attributes }}>
          {%- if block.settings.link != blank -%}
            <a class="lada__frame-a" href="{{ block.settings.link }}"
               {%- if link_name != blank %} aria-label="{{ link_name | escape }}"{% endif -%}>
              {{- frame_media -}}
            </a>
          {%- else -%}
            {{- frame_media -}}
          {%- endif -%}
        </figure>
      {%- endfor -%}
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      Narrative goes BELOW the last frame and nowhere else. A paragraph
      dropped between two photographs turns a lookbook back into a
      landing page, which is the one thing this section exists not to be.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- liquid
      assign note_kicker = section.settings.kicker
      assign note_heading = section.settings.heading
      assign note_text = section.settings.text
      assign note_label = section.settings.link_label
    -%}
    {%- if note_kicker != blank or note_heading != blank or note_text != blank or note_label != blank -%}
      <div class="lada__wrap lada__sec lada__lookbook-note">
        {%- if note_kicker != blank -%}
          <p class="lada__kicker">{{ note_kicker }}</p>
        {%- endif -%}
        {%- if note_heading != blank -%}
          <h2 class="lada__h2">{{ note_heading }}</h2>
        {%- endif -%}
        {%- if note_text != blank -%}
          <p class="lada__p">{{ note_text }}</p>
        {%- endif -%}
        {%- if note_label != blank -%}
          <a class="lada__link" href="{{ section.settings.link_url | default: '/collections/lada' }}">{{ note_label }}</a>
        {%- endif -%}
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}
  </section>
{%- endif -%}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Lada lookbook",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "A run of full-bleed photographs with no captions, prices or buttons. Each frame is a block. On the live page the section stays completely hidden until at least one frame has a photograph, so it is safe to leave the frames empty until the shoot is in. Here in the editor the empty frames stay visible so you can fill and reorder them."
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "cap_height",
      "label": "Crop tall frames to the height of the screen",
      "default": false,
      "info": "Off, a portrait frame runs at its full 2:3 height, which is taller than a desktop screen. On, every frame stops at one screen and the photograph is cropped to fit."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "region_label",
      "label": "Section name for screen readers",
      "default": "Lada lookbook"
    },
    { "type": "header", "content": "Narrative, below the last frame" },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Leave every field here blank to end the page on a photograph."
    },
    { "type": "text", "id": "kicker", "label": "Kicker", "default": "The lookbook" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "heading", "label": "Heading", "default": "Cut, and then worn" },
    {
      "type": "textarea",
      "id": "text",
      "label": "Text",
      "default": "Photographed as the pieces leave the workshop, on the men they were measured for. Nothing here is styled with anything we do not sell."
    },
    { "type": "text", "id": "link_label", "label": "Link label", "default": "The full range" },
    { "type": "url", "id": "link_url", "label": "Link" }
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "frame",
      "name": "Frame",
      "settings": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": "One photograph, edge to edge. On the live page this frame is skipped entirely until a picture is chosen."
        },
        { "type": "image_picker", "id": "image", "label": "Photograph" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "asset_file",
          "label": "…or theme asset filename",
          "info": "Used only when no photograph is picked. Type the filename exactly as it is uploaded, or the frame shows a broken picture."
        },
        {
          "type": "select",
          "id": "ratio",
          "label": "Shape",
          "default": "portrait",
          "options": [
            { "value": "portrait", "label": "Portrait 2:3" },
            { "value": "landscape", "label": "Landscape 3:2" }
          ],
          "info": "Alternate them. Portraits carry the figure, landscapes let the run breathe."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "alt",
          "label": "Alt text",
          "info": "What the photograph shows, for screen readers and for a slow connection. Leave blank only if the frame says nothing the page has not already said in words."
        },
        {
          "type": "url",
          "id": "link",
          "label": "Link to a product",
          "info": "Optional. The frame becomes clickable with no visible button, arrow or label."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "link_label",
          "label": "Link name for screen readers",
          "info": "Used only when the frame is linked and the alt text is blank. Name the piece itself, for example 'Ivory silk waistcoat', so two linked frames never read out the same."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "max_blocks": 9,
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "Lada lookbook",
      "blocks": [
        { "type": "frame", "settings": { "ratio": "portrait" } },
        { "type": "frame", "settings": { "ratio": "portrait" } },
        { "type": "frame", "settings": { "ratio": "landscape" } },
        { "type": "frame", "settings": { "ratio": "portrait" } },
        { "type": "frame", "settings": { "ratio": "portrait" } },
        { "type": "frame", "settings": { "ratio": "portrait" } },
        { "type": "frame", "settings": { "ratio": "landscape" } },
        { "type": "frame", "settings": { "ratio": "portrait" } }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
